   "faq": [
    {
     "q": "What is the difference between an SOP and a work instruction?",
     "a": "An SOP describes a process, its purpose and responsibilities. A work instruction describes how one person performs one task within that process, step by step."
    },
    {
     "q": "How long should a work instruction be?",
     "a": "As short as possible while still complete. Most tasks fit in 5 to 15 steps. Split longer tasks into separate instructions."
    },
    {
     "q": "Who should write work instructions?",
     "a": "The process owner or engineer writes them together with an experienced operator. The operator brings the real-world knowledge; the writer brings structure."
    },
    {
     "q": "How often should you update work instructions?",
     "a": "After every change to the machine, product or process, and at least during each periodic review. Digital instructions make it easier because there is only one live version."
    }
   ],
